= Wave 13 compiled context =

'''State: Phase 2 — Context compilation + writing.'''

Wave 13 extends the existing network, distributed-system, security, identity, governance, and Semantic Web corpus into the durable Internet/Web/platform layer.

== Normalized definitions ==

=== Internet ===
The Internet is a global system of interconnected computer networks that communicate using the Internet protocol suite.

=== World Wide Web ===
The World Wide Web is an information system of interrelated resources identified by URIs and accessed through Web protocols and representations.

=== Protocol (computing) ===
A protocol in computing is a defined set of rules, message structures, state transitions, and conventions that allows communicating systems to coordinate.

=== Internet protocol suite ===
The Internet protocol suite is the family of interoperating protocols used to communicate across the Internet, commonly organized into link, Internet, transport, and application layers.

=== Domain Name System ===
The Domain Name System (DNS) is a distributed hierarchical naming system that maps domain names to resource records used by Internet applications.

=== Uniform Resource Identifier ===
A Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) is a compact identifier for a resource defined according to a URI scheme.

=== Hypertext Transfer Protocol ===
The Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) is a stateless application-layer protocol with extensible semantics for interacting with networked resources.

=== Client-server model ===
The client-server model is a distributed-computing pattern in which a client initiates requests for services or resources and a server provides responses or services.

=== Web browser ===
A web browser is a user-agent application that retrieves, interprets, presents, and interacts with Web resources.

=== Web server ===
A web server is software or a network service that accepts Web requests and returns HTTP responses for resources it is authorized to serve.

=== Application programming interface ===
An application programming interface (API) is a defined interface through which software components expose operations, data, or services to other software.

=== Authentication ===
Authentication is the process of establishing confidence that a claimant controls or corresponds to an asserted identity, account, credential, or authenticator under a defined system.

=== Authorization ===
Authorization is the process or policy decision that determines whether a subject is permitted to perform an action on a resource under defined rules.

=== Interoperability ===
Interoperability is the ability of distinct systems or components to exchange information or services and use the exchanged results according to shared expectations.

=== Federation (information technology) ===
Federation in information technology is an arrangement in which independently administered systems interoperate through shared protocols, trust relationships, or exchange rules while retaining separate control.

=== Online platform ===
An online platform is a digital service that provides a shared technical and governance environment in which users, organizations, software, content, or transactions can interact.

=== Content moderation ===
Content moderation is the set of processes by which a platform or community applies rules to user-generated content, accounts, or interactions.

== Required non-equivalences ==

<pre>
Internet ≠ World Wide Web
protocol ≠ implementation
Internet protocol suite ≠ Internet
DNS ≠ Web
URI ≠ network location necessarily
HTTP ≠ HTML
HTTP ≠ Web
client/server role ≠ permanent machine identity
browser ≠ search engine
web server ≠ physical machine necessarily
API ≠ protocol necessarily
authentication ≠ authorization
interoperability ≠ identical implementation
federation ≠ decentralization
federation ≠ single sign-on
online platform ≠ Internet
moderation action ≠ proof of falsity
</pre>

== Reader-linking rule ==

Core pages link only to canonical Core identities that already exist or are created in this wave. Simple/Technical surfaces remain derivatives and do not duplicate Knowledge object/category identity.

== Standards-state rule ==

References distinguish IETF RFCs, W3C Recommendations, and final NIST publications from drafts or general literature. NIST SP 800-63 Revision 4 is cited as final July 2025 guidance; W3C ActivityPub is cited as a 23 January 2018 Recommendation.

== Redlink policy ==

Future descendants captured in Phase 1 remain coverage signals, not automatic stub authorization.

